$\Delta G_R(P, T)$ contour maps, in particular those representing isoenergy lines, are useful graphical constructs. Information such as spontaneity, the thermal character  (e.g., whether the reaction is endothermic or exothermic), the entropic character of a reaction (e.g., whether the reaction produces or consumes entropy) and even the equilibrium constant can be easily obtained just by inspecting the map. 
Perhaps teh easiest reaction characteristic that can be obtained from the $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ contour maps is spontaneity. The reaction is nonspontaneous under the temperature and pressure conditions represented in point \emph{c} of Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (left panel), as $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ is positive. We say that the reaction is \emph{endergonic}. Differently, under the temperature and pressure conditions represented in point \emph{d} the reaction will proceed spontaneously as $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ is now negative and the reaction is now \emph{exergonic}. 
The curvature of the equilibrium isoline informs about the thermal character of a reaction. The reaction depicted in Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (left panel) is endothermic, as the equilibrium isoline bends upwards, whereas the reaction depicted in Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (right panel) is exothermic, as the equilibrium isoline bends downwards.
The Asymptotic values of the equilibrium isoline for large temperatures inform about the entropic character of a reaction. We can see in Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (left panel) that the equilibrium isoline tends to a positive value ($\sim$3) at very large temperatures, hence the reaction will produce entropy. Differently, the reaction depicted in Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (right panel) consumes entropy as the equilibrium isoline tends to a negative value ($-\sim$3) at very large temperatures.
We can also ontain $K_c$ for different tempeature using the equilibrium isoline. Remember that $K_c$ changes with temperature and hence there is not a single value for $K_c$ for a reaction. We can, for example, obtain $K_c$ for standard temperature conditions (T=298K, the temperature at which all thermodynamic data is tabulated) as shown in Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines}. The equilibrium isoline either increases or decreases with temperature. 
As we described, the value of the isoline at fixed conditions informs about spontaneity, whereas the curvature of the equilibrium isoline informs about enthalpy, and the asymptotic values of the equilibrium isoline informs about entropy.